ng the c3 square for the knight), and creates a reversed Benoni-like structure which is comfortable for Black. Defending with c6 is solid but passive. Capturing c4 cedes central control.\nReason: Pushing d5-d4 takes advantage of White's slow play (g3) to claim a space advantage. It solves the tension on d5 by advancing past the attacking pawn and disrupts White's coordination.\nAnswer: P: d5-d4"}
